Title: The Silence
Order: 2
Production Code: 1x02

Cast: JIM DAVIS, THE NARRATOR. (All other voices are the dead, who do not speak.) Running time target: 12-15 minutes.

PRODUCTION NOTE: This episode is mostly sound and no speech. The sound designer owns it. Dialogue is sparse and always near the microphone; the silence between lines is written as AMB and SFX cues so that it is performed, not left empty.

INT. THE LOWER VAULTS - THE SUB-VAULT STAIR - LATER

AMB: a faint, wet drip; below hearing, a pressure of stone

SFX: Jim's breath, slow, returning; a groan as he wakes

JIM DAVIS
(a gasp)
Oh --

SFX: he leaps clear of the body; boots scraping stone; the lantern clatters

NARRATOR
He awoke with a sense of horror, leaped from the body, and groped up the stairs, calling to the guard.

SFX: stairs, quick, stumbling; his voice carrying up the stairwell

JIM DAVIS
(calling)
Guard! Guard!

SFX: an iron gate swinging on a hinge, swinging back, swinging again, not latched

NARRATOR
The watchman sat as if asleep, with the gate swinging free.

JIM DAVIS
(looking at him, low)
Watchman?

SFX: one glance; then the stairs again, faster

INT. THE SUB-VAULT

AMB: huge, echoing space; each footstep comes back as another

JIM DAVIS
(shouting into it)
Hello! Hello! Anyone!

SFX: his voice echoes and re-echoes weirdly, losing its own words

NARRATOR
In vain he called to the guards. His voice echoed and re-echoed weirdly.

INT. THE BANK - THE BASEMENT

SFX: running up a ramp; the floor is concrete; an echo

SFX: a body shifted by a boot; a guard lying prostrate on his face

JIM DAVIS (V.O.)
(cold dread rising)
Cold. Still.

NARRATOR
A fear arose in the messenger's heart.

SFX: he runs, steps hitting stairs two at a time

MUSIC: BED IN - a thin, high tone, like air in a bottle; no melody

INT. THE BANK - MAIN FLOOR

AMB: the bank's marble hall, dead silent. No clock ticking. No pens. No typewriter. A shift of paper on a draft - that is all.

SFX: the clock on the wall has stopped - we hear the last, slowest tick, then nothing

Hold the silence a long time. Then:

NARRATOR
The stillness of death lay everywhere, and everywhere bowed, bent, and stretched the silent forms of men.

SFX: Jim's steps, small now on the marble; he stops; he turns slowly

NARRATOR (CONT'D)
The messenger paused and glanced about. He was not a man easily moved. But the sight was appalling.

SFX: the creak of a chair as he gets close to the president's desk

JIM DAVIS
(a whisper, to himself)
Robbery and murder.

SFX: the soft knock of the desk's edge against his thigh; a pen rolls off and clatters

JIM DAVIS (V.O.)
(slow, calculating, afraid)
If they found him here, alone - with all this money, and all these dead men - what would his life be worth?

MUSIC: STING - a single plucked note, close

SFX: soft steps, tiptoeing; he glances behind him; looks again

SFX: a latch turned, quietly; a side door opens on a squeak

EXT. WALL STREET - HIGH NOON

SFX: the door closes behind him. Very softly.

AMB: nothing. Faint wind. Flags flapping somewhere. A distant gull.

NARRATOR
How silent the street was.

JIM DAVIS
(low, wondering)
Not a soul stirring. And yet it is high noon.

SFX: he turns; looks up; the long echo of one footstep on the stone

JIM DAVIS (CONT'D)
(a stammer)
Wall Street? Broadway?

SFX: he steps back until his shoulder meets a wall; a stifled cry

NARRATOR
In the great stone doorway, a hundred men and women and children lay crushed and twisted and jammed, forced into that great gaping doorway like refuse in a can.

SFX: a long, quiet creak as a body shifts under its own weight, settles; no one moves

JIM DAVIS
(choking cry, then low)
No. No.

NARRATOR
As if in one wild, frantic rush to safety, they had rushed and ground themselves to death.

SFX: his hand dragging along the wall; slow steps; he is wetting his dry lips

SFX: a silk-hatted figure standing stone dead by the wall - a gust moves a coat-tail; Jim takes in the sound of a stiff coat, a frock coat, a hat with a faint tick as it shifts

NARRATOR (CONT'D)
He met a business man, silk-hatted and frock-coated, who had crept too along that smooth wall, and stood now stone dead with wonder written on his lips.

SFX: Jim's steps leave; he moves toward the curb

SFX: a woman's dress rustles against a signpost as the wind lifts it; a street car stands with its door open, its bell silent

NARRATOR (CONT'D)
A woman leaned wearily against the signpost, her head bowed motionless on her lace and silken bosom. Before her stood a street car, silent, and within -

SFX: a glance, a catch of breath; steps quickly away

NARRATOR (CONT'D)
But the messenger but glanced, and hurried on.

SFX: a rustle of newsprint in a gutter; a boy's wrist; the paper held up in the wind

NARRATOR (CONT'D)
A grimy newsboy sat in the gutter with the last edition in his uplifted hand.

SFX: the paper flaps once

MUSIC: SOURCE BED IN - from far away, a barrel organ playing a sentimental waltz that has wound down to a stop and a stutter

Jim lifts the paper from the dead hand. We hear it crackle as he reads. Over the scene, a newsboy's call plays, remembered; it does not come from any living mouth:

NEWSBOY (V.O.)
(a recorded cry, from earlier that morning)
Extra! Extra! Comet's tail sweeps past at noon!

JIM DAVIS
(reading aloud, slowly)
"Danger!" - screamed its black headlines. "Warnings wired around the world. The Comet's tail sweeps past us at noon. Deadly gases expected. Close doors and windows. Seek the cellar."

SFX: the paper falls from his hand; scrapes along the pavement

JIM DAVIS (CONT'D)
(a short, bitter breath)
Seek the cellar.

NARRATOR
The messenger read, and staggered on.

SFX: his steps, unsteady; a stumble; a recovery

NARRATOR (CONT'D)
Far out from a window above, a girl lay with gasping face and sleevelets on her arms. On a store step sat a little, sweet-faced girl looking upward toward the skies, and in the carriage by her lay -

SFX: Jim's steps stop - and then, "but the messenger looked no longer"

NARRATOR (CONT'D)
But the messenger looked no longer.

MUSIC: BED IN - the thin tone from before breaks upward into a rising, shaking string line

SFX: a sob that is almost a scream, torn out of him

NARRATOR (CONT'D)
The cords gave way. The terror burst in his veins, and with one great gasping cry he sprang desperately forward and ran.

SFX: a man running, then running harder - a blur of footsteps, ragged breath, a shout, the sound of his own arms beating the air

JIM DAVIS
(shrieking, in his own voice, hardly human)
Ah! Ah! Ah!

SFX: running through an empty city; each echo comes back late

SFX: the last wail of pain; a body falling on grass

MUSIC: OUT - dead stop

EXT. MADISON SQUARE - CONTINUOUS

AMB: wind in trees; birdsong, a thin pip of it, and no more

SFX: a long, ragged sigh as he lies still

Hold. A full ten seconds of nothing but grass and breath.

SFX: a body gets to its feet; steps across gravel; past benches. He does not look.

NARRATOR
When he rose, he gave no glance at the still and silent forms on the benches.

SFX: a fountain, still running - water falling into a stone basin

AMB: water, steady, a clean sound after so much dead air

SFX: he splashes his face, a hand scooping water, again

JIM DAVIS
(breath coming under control)
Think it through.

NARRATOR
Hiding himself in a corner away from the drama of death, he quietly gripped himself and thought the thing through.

JIM DAVIS (V.O.)
(spoken calmly, almost clinically)
The comet had swept the earth, and this was the end. Was everybody dead?

SFX: a long pause, his breathing

JIM DAVIS
(aloud, firm)
I must search and see.

NARRATOR
He knew that he must steady himself, and keep calm, or he would go insane.

JIM DAVIS (V.O.)
(a thought that arrives quietly)
And the gold. The chest. The gold, in the dark, for a hundred years, and no one to ever take it.

A flicker; nothing more, and he lets it go.

JIM DAVIS (CONT'D)
(a dry breath)
First, I must go to a restaurant.

EXT. FIFTH AVENUE - THE HOSTELRY

AMB: wide avenue, empty; a sign creaks on its chain

SFX: Jim's steps, long, up the avenue; the city is only wind

NARRATOR
He walked up Fifth Avenue to a famous hostelry, and entered its gorgeous, ghost-haunted halls.

SFX: a heavy door; carpeted hall; chandeliers tinkling softly in a draft

AMB: a long, lofty room with cutlery on plates and a hundred places set; a dead room tone, slightly sweet, slightly wrong

SFX: Jim beating back nausea; a swallow, a gag; he steadies himself

SFX: a tray lifted from dead hands, silver on china

NARRATOR (CONT'D)
He beat back the nausea, and, seizing a tray from dead hands, hurried into the street.

EXT. THE HOSTELRY - STEPS

SFX: he sits on the step, the tray on his knees; a fork on a plate

SFX: eating - quickly, then ravenously, the sound of a man eating ravenously

JIM DAVIS
(a whisper, through a full mouth)
Yesterday, they would not have served me.

NARRATOR
He ate ravenously, hiding to keep out the sights.

SFX: he forces the last of it down; sets the tray aside with great care

MUSIC: BED IN - a lonely clarinet over a low drone, slow, with a trace of ragtime far under it, like a half-remembered song

EXT. NEW YORK STREETS - THE SEARCH

SFX: he picks up a dead telephone in a lobby; dial tone: nothing. He clicks the hook. Nothing.

SFX: a fire-alarm box, its glass breaking, its handle pulled; the clack of a clapper far off; no bell answers

JIM DAVIS
(low, to the street)
Hello? Hello?

NARRATOR
Then he started up the street, looking, peering, telephoning, ringing alarms.

NARRATOR (CONT'D)
Silent, silent all.

SFX: a lonely, long note held, then let go

JIM DAVIS (V.O.)
(trailing off)
Was nobody - nobody - he dared not think the thought.

SFX: his steps hurry on

SFX: he stops short

JIM DAVIS
(a low, wild laugh)
The subway.

SFX: a short, cracked laugh that dies as soon as it starts

JIM DAVIS (CONT'D)
(to himself)
No. A car. If I can find a Ford.

SFX: his footsteps in a run; the clink of keys; a car door; a body gently lifted and laid on the pavement - the care in it audible

SFX: the car door shutting, hollow and metal

SFX: a Ford's starter; throttle tested; the engine catches, coughs, settles

JIM DAVIS
(a grim breath)
There was gas.

MUSIC: BED OUT

EXT. FIFTH AVENUE - THE DRIVE NORTH

AMB: wide empty city wind; an engine, steady, the only sound in a hundred blocks

SFX: the Ford, gliding along; a gear change

NARRATOR
He glided off, shivering, and drove up the street.

SFX: the car passing a wrecked automobile - metal ticking as it cools; the Ford slows, goes on

NARRATOR (CONT'D)
Everywhere stood, leaned, lounged, and lay the dead, in grim and awful silence.

SFX: another car, filled with a gay party; the sound of a gramophone, running down, one long note falling

NARRATOR (CONT'D)
Past another, filled with a gay party whose smiles yet lingered on their death-struck lips.

SFX: a police whistle on a lanyard swinging; a dead policeman's boot, scraped aside; Jim eases the Ford past

SFX: a long loop of the engine as he threads through traffic jammed solid

JIM DAVIS
(grim)
Forty-second Street. Dead congestion. Park Avenue.

SFX: the car turning; a different road surface, a longer echo

SFX: back on Fifth Avenue - the engine opening up, a rush of wind

NARRATOR
He came back on Fifth Avenue at 57th and flew past the Plaza, and by the park with its hushed babies and silent throng.

AMB: wind through the park, trees, a leaf blowing - no voices, no prams

SFX: the Ford at speed, tires whining on pavement

NARRATOR (CONT'D)
Until, as he was rushing past 72nd Street -

SFX: the engine; wind; the rhythm of the street

SFX: faint, far, and clear - a human voice

MUSIC: STING - the only note in the episode that is warm; a single violin, played the way a voice is

WOMAN (O.S.)
(far, desperate, cracked)
Hello! Hello! Help, in God's name!

SFX: the Ford's brakes; a skid; it comes to a stop; the engine idling

NARRATOR
He heard a sharp cry, and saw a living form leaning wildly out an upper window.

JIM DAVIS
(under his breath, a gasp)
God.

NARRATOR
He gasped. The human voice sounded in his ears like the voice of God.

WOMAN (O.S.)
(wailing)
There's a dead girl in here, and a man, and - and see yonder, dead men lying in the street, and dead horses -

SFX: the Ford's engine snaps forward; he swings it round in a circle

MUSIC: OUT
